soc: sunxi: cpu_comm: service every msgbox RX endpoint, name the banks

Every boot prints two errors:

    hy310-cpu-comm 3003000.cpu-comm: error -ENXIO: IRQ index 1 not found
    hy310-cpu-comm 3003000.cpu-comm: error -ENXIO: IRQ index 2 not found

The probe asks for three interrupts, platform_get_irq(pdev, 0..2), because
the msgbox has three GIC lines; our device tree node gave it one. Patch
0024d puts all three into the node. This patch makes the driver side of
that honest: the handler must work no matter which of the three lines it
was called for, and the log must say which line is which bank.

Line to bank, and what the driver owns on each
----------------------------------------------
One GIC line per register bank, in the order the stock device tree lists
them (node msgbox@3003000: reg 0x3003000/0x3003400/0x3003800, 0x400 each;
interrupts <0 0x2E 4  0 0x6D 4  0 0x6C 4>; msgbox_amp_counts = 3,
msgbox_amp_local = 0):

    index 0   bank 0 @0x03003000   this core's inbox      GIC_SPI 46
    index 1   bank 1 @0x03003400   CPUS/ARISC inbox       GIC_SPI 109
    index 2   bank 2 @0x03003800   display MIPS inbox     GIC_SPI 108

The pairing is positional. The vendor driver builds base[i] from reg[i]
(devm_platform_ioremap_resource in a loop) and irq[i] from interrupt i
(of_irq_get in a loop over of_irq_count) and never reorders either
(sunxi_msgbox_probe at 0xc07db360, IDA database
analyse/ida/db-audio-vmlinux/vmlinux.elf.i64, vendor arm32 vmlinux).

Which registers sit in which bank is the vendor's own arithmetic. With
sub(R, S) = (R < S) ? S - 1 : S, the whole register block of a link from
sender S to receiver R sits in R's bank at 0x100 * sub(R, S):

    RX enable   +0x20   BIT(2 * port)        sx_read_irq_enable  0xc07daab8
    RX status   +0x24   write 1 to clear     sunxi_msgbox_irq    0xc07dacc0
    TX enable   +0x30   BIT(2 * port + 1)    sx_write_irq_disable 0xc07daaf0
    TX status   +0x34   write 1 to clear     sunxi_msgbox_irq    0xc07dacc0
    FIFO count  +0x60 + 4 * port             sunxi_msgbox_irq    0xc07dacc0
    word        +0x70 + 4 * port             sunxi_msgbox_irq    0xc07dacc0
    bank(R)     = 0x03003000 + 0x400 * R     sx_base             0xc07dabc8

With ARM = user 0, ARISC = user 1, display MIPS = user 2 that reproduces
every msgbox address this project has rung on hardware, which is why the
mapping can be trusted:

    MIPS -> ARM   bank 0, sub(0,2)=1  0x120/0x124/0x164/0x174  (this driver)
    ARISC -> ARM  bank 0, sub(0,1)=0  0x060 + 4*ch / 0x070 + 4*ch
                                      (h713-arisc, reg-name "msgbox-rx")
    ARM -> MIPS   bank 2, sub(2,0)=0  0x830/0x864/0x874        (this driver)
    ARM -> ARISC  bank 1, sub(1,0)=0  0x430/0x470/0x47c
                                      (this driver and h713-arisc)

So this core receives in bank 0 only; banks 1 and 2 are the other two
cores' inboxes and hold only the TX side of our two outgoing links. The
send path pulses the TX enable and clears it again ten microseconds later
(set -> udelay(10) -> 0), so neither of those two lines can be left
asserted by us.

What changes
------------
The handler no longer hard-codes one endpoint. It walks a table of the RX
endpoints this driver owns and does the proven mask -> drain -> clear ->
unmask sequence on each, in that order, unchanged. The table has one entry
today, so the behaviour on SPI 46 is exactly what it was; the point is
that lines 109 and 108 now run the same loop instead of a hard-coded bank.
Bank 0 sub-block 0 is the ARISC's answer FIFO and belongs to the arisc
driver -- it is deliberately not in the table, or cpu_comm would eat the
ARISC's replies.

IRQ_NONE when nothing was drained stays as it is (the 2026-09-01 interrupt
storm; the comment in the handler explains it). A line that fires for
someone else stays visible to the kernel instead of being swallowed.

The vendor handler solves the same problem the same way: sunxi_msgbox_irq
ignores its irq argument entirely and walks every endpoint on its list, so
one handler serves all three lines. It differs in one respect worth
stating -- it returns IRQ_HANDLED unconditionally. We do not.

The probe's log lines now name the bank behind each index, and so does the
per-line message in cpu_comm_msgbox_request_irq(), so a boot log says
which of the three lines came up and which did not.

Sources: material/stock-hy310-board.dts (decompiled stock device tree),
the IDA database named above, doku/61 B, doku/82 (the arisc driver's two
msgbox windows), doku/125 section 3.

